        <Item class="ModuleScript" referent="21">
          <Properties>
            <string name="Name">Version</string>
            <BinaryString name="AttributesSerialize" />
            <SecurityCapabilities name="Capabilities">0</SecurityCapabilities>
            <ContentId name="LinkedSource">
              <null>
              </null>
            </ContentId>
            <bool name="DefinesCapabilities">false</bool>
            <BinaryString name="SerializedOverrides">
            </BinaryString>
            <string name="Source">-- Kernel semver. Single source of truth - the server stamps it as the
-- RBLXE_KernelVersion attribute on ReplicatedStorage.RBLXEssentials at boot,
-- and the Dev Plugin's ExporterV2 reads it through
-- `PackFormat.parseKernelVersion` to stamp the same attribute on every
-- exported RBLXE_Pack container.
--
-- BUMP THIS WITH EVERY KERNEL CHANGE. The kernel unit is shipped inside each
-- pack, and `Installer.decideKernel` replaces an installed kernel ONLY when
-- the incoming version is strictly greater - an equal version is
-- "kept-existing". So a kernel fix that does not raise this number reaches
-- nobody: reinstalling the pack silently keeps the old modules. Learned the
-- hard way on 2026-08-24, when the panels-above-HUD and UI_Blocker fixes
-- both looked like they had not worked after a reinstall (user: "he
-- reinstalado el roulette system en el place de prueba y nada, se ve por
-- detrás el fondo aún").
--
-- 2.0.1 - panels draw above the HUD (Shell ZIndex ranks); UI_Blocker tag
--         skips wiring for a modal's click-eating sheet.
-- 2.0.2 - MenuSystem:getInteractable(panelName): the HUD button a pack's
--         notification badge belongs on. Additive, so an older pack keeps
--         working against this kernel unchanged - but a pack that ASKS for it
--         needs this version present, or it falls back to scanning.
-- 2.1.0 - ctx.kernel.Notify: the notification facade, shaped like SFX. A
--         no-op until Notifications System v2 registers a backend, so every
--         pack can announce unconditionally without declaring a dependency
--         on an optional pack. MINOR, not patch: this adds API that packs
--         built against it will call, and an older kernel does not have it.
-- 2.1.1 - menus and HUD buttons survive character respawn: the server makes
--         StarterGui.RBLXEssentials a ScreenGui with ResetOnSpawn = false at
--         boot (UI/RootPersistence.lua). Before, every respawn destroyed every
--         panel and HUD button MenuSystem had adopted. PATCH: no API change.
-- 3.0.0 - RBLX Essentials 3.0 foundation (design spec 2026-09-23):
--         game-owned configuration (ctx.config from each pack's Schema and
--         the game's Config modules; Schema.lua, Config.lua), interface piece
--         contracts (Contracts.lua) and piece resolution decided on the server
--         and enforced on clients (UI/Pieces.lua, RBLXE_PieceWinners,
--         MenuSystem pieces gate, getPiece, ctx.getPiece). The respawn fix
--         (StarterGui.RBLXEssentials made a persistent ScreenGui at boot, so
--         the pieces a client adopted survive respawns) is not new in 3.0: it
--         shipped in 2.1.1 (UI/RootPersistence.lua) and 3.0 keeps it. The
--         client now waits for game.Loaded
--         before booting. Final A1 fix wave (still 3.0.0, unreleased): a
--         config load that throws fails only its pack, Schema.check rejects
--         unusable option types, a chosen piece takes its MenuSystem slot
--         over any 2.x copy, contracts check what MenuSystem needs (tags,
--         PanelId, TargetPanel, `opens`), and clients retire only the
--         StarterGui copies the server refused, never a pack's own clones.
--         A2a hardening for Tools (still 3.0.0, unreleased): Schema.check
--         walks whole patterns and checks list, map and optional-group
--         defaults; Contracts.check reports unusable specs and roots as
--         problems; Config.load reports a wrong side, packId, schemaLib or
--         module argument as an error. Callers keep their pcall for the
--         rest (Loader.lua does).
--         A2b (still 3.0.0): Schema.describe rows also carry optional,
--         value, example and constraints, for config templates and Tools.
--         A3 (still 3.0.0): Schema refuses defaults under server-only
--         options (RT-10) and takes check/checkDoc rules; getPiece counts
--         from PlayerGui.RBLXEssentials' arrival and answers an undecided id
--         at once; the piece runtime warns where it was silent (T-G, M-b,
--         M-c, M-d); Interface/Custom is never a candidate; an invalid
--         Pieces declaration fails its pack (RT-6); clients skip the packs
--         the server could not start (RBLXE_ServerReport) and Net.client
--         waits can be bounded (RT-5).
--         B0 (still 3.0.0, then frozen): Schema kinds S.color and S.tree
--         and the pinned flag; ctx.companion, ctx.extension(s) (the game's
--         Extensions zone) and no ctx.settings for a pack with a Schema
--         (a notice for the others); the Layer piece kind.
--         MAJOR: official 2.x packs are replaced together
--         with it at release; during development 2.x packs keep ctx.settings
--         and 2.x adoption.
-- 3.0.1 - piece specs may declare standsInFor = "&lt;PackId&gt;.&lt;Name&gt;": a
--         stand-in copy of a piece another pack owns (the General UI Kit
--         ships its 2.x menus this way). Pieces.plan refuses the stand-in,
--         its default and every Interface copy, when the owner piece is
--         declared, and a HudButton that opens a refused stand-in panel is
--         refused with it; without the owner the stand-in is planned like
--         any piece. An owner of another kind keeps its piece and the
--         stand-in is refused with a warning. PATCH: an older Kernel ignores
--         the field and would choose both copies, so a pack that declares
--         stand-ins needs Kernel &gt;= 3.0.1.
-- 3.2.0 - removed pieces: a declared piece with no copy is the game's choice; ctx.hasPiece.
--         Pieces.plan puts a declared piece with no copy at
--         all (no default, no Interface copy, not a stand-in its owner
--         replaced) in `removed`, prints "removed by the game: &lt;ids&gt;" and
--         never warns about it; the decision carries the ids, so the
--         client's getPiece answers nil at once without a warning, and
--         Runtime:isRemoved / Runtime:has, MenuSystem:hasPiece and the
--         client's ctx.hasPiece(name) let a pack skip a piece the game
--         deleted. MINOR: an older Kernel warns "no piece available" and
--         waits on getPiece; a pack that calls ctx.hasPiece needs 3.2.0.
return "3.2.0"
