                <string name="Source">-- 3.2.2: the music stopped after each track (live 3.5.0 customer test, 26
-- Oct, both kits): Ended switched the SoundId and called Play inside the
-- same Ended handler, which Roblox ignores, so the next track stayed paused
-- at 0:00. Now Ended defers the switch to the next frame, a track that is
-- not loaded yet plays once Loaded fires, and half a second after each
-- switch a check plays it again if it still is not playing
-- (tests/client.spec.luau, "track end").
-- 454: attach returns mountGui for late engine delivery; playback starts once.
-- 3.2.1: the client can start before the Music group's tracks replicate. With
-- no track at start nothing played, and the tracks that came in later only
-- joined the playlist, so the music stayed silent until Next (live customer
-- test, 17 Oct: one fresh Play in four). Now the first track to arrive while
-- nothing plays starts the music (tests/client.spec.luau, "late tracks").
-- 432: bind only live PlayerGui views, use Activated for mouse/touch/gamepad,
-- preserve authored mute icons, and clean each removed view and ActiveTrack.
-- Historical port notes below describe the original v2 baseline.
--[[
	MusicSystem.lua - Music player with multi-skin support (kernel v2 port).

	Ported from v1 Sound System/src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/
	RBLXEssentials/SoundSystem/MusicSystem.client.lua (271 LOC - read fully
	for this port). v1 discovered player UI globally via CollectionService tag
	"MusicPlayerFrame"; every tagged frame shares one playback state (song,
	position, mute). Music tracks are Sound instances inside the Music
	SoundGroup. Expected frame structure (verified against the extracted
	MusicPlayerGUI.rbxm/Templates.rbxm assets, Task 1/2):

		Frame (tagged MusicPlayerFrame)
		└── Container
			├── SongName   (TextLabel)
			├── Duration   (TextLabel)
			└── Buttons
				├── Mute   (ImageButton with Icon child)
				└── Next   (ImageButton)

	Declared fixes vs v1 (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-06-sound-v2-
	conversion-design.md §5.1 + task-4-brief.md):
	  1. v1's `refreshAllMuteIcons`, `applyGroupVolume`, `nextTrack` were
	     declared WITHOUT `local` - accidental globals - and `registerFrame`
	     called the first two before their definitions, working only via
	     Lua's global late-binding. Here all three are forward-declared
	     locals (`local refreshAllMuteIcons` etc. up top, assigned via
	     `name = function(...)` later) so `registerFrame` closes over the
	     real locals instead of relying on globals. This is standard Lua
	     scoping: `function foo()` after `local foo` assigns the existing
	     local, not a global - used deliberately below for readability, but
	     spelled out here since it's easy to misread as re-declaring a new
	     local.
	  2. v1's top-level `if not musicPlayerEnabled.Value then ... return end`
	     (script-level early exit) becomes a guarded `attach(deps)`: when
	     `deps.enabled.Value` is false, `attach` disables the gui (if given)
	     and returns without wiring anything - same observable behavior,
	     expressed as a function guard instead of a script-level `return`.
	  3. Trove is no longer vendored - `deps.trove` is the kernel's Trove
	     instance (already constructed by the caller), matching every other
	     v2 Client module in this pack (see VolumeHandlers.lua).
	  4. `deps.gui` is the ALREADY-RESOLVED player ScreenGui instance (Task 5
	     originally cloned `Assets.MusicPlayerGUI` into PlayerGui and picked
	     the configured template before calling `attach`; Task M2,
	     2026-08-06-gui-edit-time-migration-design.md, retired the clone -
	     the GUI now ships as real StarterGui content and `Client/init.lua`
	     instead WaitForChild-resolves the StarterGui-&gt;PlayerGui copy at
	     `PlayerGui.RBLXEssentials.SoundSystem.MusicPlayerGUI` before picking
	     the template and calling `attach`, the same fixed path v1's
	     freestanding `setGuiEnabled` used to re-discover the GUI, now
	     arrived at via the engine's own StarterGui clone instead of a
	     manual one). `attach` itself is unaffected either way: it just
	     flips `.Enabled` on whatever instance it's given.

	Everything else is parity with v1: single reusable "ActiveTrack" Sound
	inside the Music group (Looped=false, so it respects group volume),
	random start track, Ended -&gt; next, ChildAdded (with the same `task.wait()`
	debounce v1 used)/ChildRemoved (remove-by-Name) playlist refresh, the
	MusicPlayerFrame tag contract with multiple frames sharing one state,
	mute via group Volume with PreMuteVolume restore +
	GetPropertyChangedSignal("Volume") sync (so an external volume change,
	e.g. from VolumeHandlers' settings UI, updates the mute icon too), a
	~5 Hz RenderStepped-throttled "M:SS - M:SS" duration display, and the
	same play/muted icon asset ids (rbxassetid://6813750121 /
	rbxassetid://6813750387).

	── PURE surface (Lune-spec'd, tests/musicsystem.spec.luau) ──────────────
	`buildPlaylist(musicGroup, activeTrackName)` and `nextIndex(current,
	count, delta)` touch no `game`/service global - only the instances/
	numbers handed to them - so they're required directly under Lune via
	`luau.load`, same convention as VolumeHandlers.snapIndex/stepVolume.

	`buildPlaylist` needs each candidate Sound's asset id to decide whether
	it's "empty" (v1's `child.SoundId ~= ""` filter). Reading that id is
	genuinely different across the two environments this module ships into:

	  - REAL ROBLOX (what actually runs in-game): `Sound.SoundId` is a live,
	    readable legacy property - v1 reads it directly and that's exactly
	    what the shipped module does too (see `getSoundUri` below).
	  - LUNE 0.10.5 (what the spec runs under): the bundled rbx-dom
	    reflection database has NO default value for `SoundId` at all, so
	    reading it off ANY Sound instance that hasn't had it explicitly SET
	    in that same process errors ("Failed to get property 'SoundId' -
	    missing default value") - verified empirically (see
	    task-2-report.md, and re-verified for this task: a fresh
	    `roblox.Instance.new("Sound")` errors on `.SoundId` read, but
	    reading `.AudioContent.Uri` on the same instance succeeds and
	    returns `nil` when unset). The canonical property in both
	    environments is `AudioContent` (a `Content` datatype with `.Uri`).

	`getSoundUri(sound)` below resolves this without special-casing the
	environment: pcall-try `.SoundId` first (works unconditionally on real
	Roblox; works under Lune too IF the fixture explicitly set `.SoundId`,
	which Lune's reflection gap only blocks for property GETS with no prior
	SET - verified empirically), and only if that pcall fails or returns ""
	fall back to `.AudioContent.Uri`. This means the Lune spec below can
	exercise ALL THREE resolution paths (SoundId set -&gt; used directly;
	SoundId never set but AudioContent set via `Content.fromUri` -&gt; falls
	back correctly; neither set -&gt; resolves to "" and gets excluded), not
	just the AudioContent fallback - see musicsystem.spec.luau's "getSoundUri
	dual-environment resolution" section.
]]

local MusicSystem = {}

local ICONS = {
	Playing = "rbxassetid://6813750121",
	Muted = "rbxassetid://6813750387",
}

-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- PURE HELPERS (no `game`/service access - Lune-spec'd)
-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

--- Resolves a Sound's asset uri across both shipping environments (see the
--- module header for the full explanation). Never errors: any failure to
--- read either property resolves to `""`, matching v1's exclusion filter.
function MusicSystem.getSoundUri(sound: Instance): string
	local okId, id = pcall(function()
		return (sound :: any).SoundId
	end)
	if okId and id ~= nil and id ~= "" then
		return id
	end

	local okContent, content = pcall(function()
		return (sound :: any).AudioContent
	end)
	if okContent and content ~= nil then
		return content.Uri or ""
	end

	return ""
end

--- Builds the playlist from `musicGroup`'s current children: every `Sound`
--- except `activeTrackName` (the shared "ActiveTrack" player, excluded by
--- name - v1 excluded it by instance identity, but the pure signature here
--- only receives the group, so name is the stable, Lune-testable
--- equivalent: "ActiveTrack" is a fixed, attach-assigned name never reused
--- by an actual track) with a non-empty resolved uri (v1's
--- `child.SoundId ~= ""` filter, via `getSoundUri`). Order matches
--- `musicGroup:GetChildren()` (v1 parity - no re-sorting). Returns `{}` for
--- an empty/track-less group (no special-cased nil - an empty array is
--- already a valid "no songs" playlist for callers to check via `#`).
function MusicSystem.buildPlaylist(musicGroup: Instance, activeTrackName: string): { Instance }
	local playlist = {}
	for _, child in ipairs(musicGroup:GetChildren()) do
		if child:IsA("Sound") and child.Name ~= activeTrackName and MusicSystem.getSoundUri(child) ~= "" then
			table.insert(playlist, child)
		end
	end
	return playlist
end

--- Wraps `current + delta` into the 1-based range `[1, count]` (v1's
--- `clampIndex(i, n) = ((i - 1) % n) + 1`, generalized to take the step in
--- the same call instead of requiring the caller to pre-add it - v1's
--- `nextTrack(step)` did `playIndex(State.Index + step)`, then `playIndex`
--- clamped; here that's one call: `nextIndex(State.Index, count, step)`).
--- `delta` defaults to `1` (v1's `nextTrack(step)` treated a missing step
--- the same way). Wraps in BOTH directions: stepping past the end wraps to
--- the start and vice versa (plain modulo arithmetic, no special-casing per
--- direction). An empty playlist (`count &lt;= 0`) has no valid index to wrap
--- into, so this returns `nil` (declared v2 behavior - cleaner than v1's
--- `clampIndex(i, 0) == 1`, which only ever "worked" because v1's own
--- `playIndex` special-cased `#State.Songs == 0` BEFORE calling it; making
--- that case explicit here means `attach`'s `playIndex` can check `nil`
--- directly instead of relying on an incidental index-1-into-nothing).
function MusicSystem.nextIndex(current: number, count: number, delta: number?): number?
	if count &lt;= 0 then
		return nil
	end
	local step = delta or 1
	return ((current - 1 + step) % count) + 1
end

-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- IMPURE: attach (Studio-only - needs `game`, CollectionService,
-- RunService, GetPropertyChangedSignal, Instance.new, task.wait; none
-- Lune-testable, same convention as VolumeHandlers.attach - see
-- tests/musicsystem.spec.luau header and task-3-report.md)
-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

local function formatMMSS(sec: number): string
	sec = math.max(0, math.floor(tonumber(sec) or 0))
	return string.format("%d:%02d", math.floor(sec / 60), sec % 60)
end

--- Wires the full v1 MusicSystem behavior. `deps = { musicGroup: Instance,
--- gui: Instance?, trove: any, enabled: BoolValue }`.
---
--- Guarded-attach gate (declared fix #2, see module header): if
--- `deps.enabled.Value` is false, this disables `deps.gui` (when given) and
--- returns immediately - no ActiveTrack Sound is created, no connections
--- are made, mirroring v1's script-level `return` with zero observable
--- difference to a player.
function MusicSystem.attach(deps: {
	musicGroup: Instance,
	gui: Instance?,
	trove: any,
	enabled: Instance,
})
	local musicGroup = deps.musicGroup
	local gui = deps.gui
	local trove = deps.trove
	local enabledValue = (deps.enabled :: any).Value
	local alive = true
	trove:Add(function() alive = false end)
	local function mountGui(target)
		if not alive then return false end
		if gui == target then return true end
		if gui ~= nil then return false end
		gui = target
		;(gui :: any).Enabled = enabledValue
		return true
	end

	if gui then
		(gui :: any).Enabled = enabledValue
	end

	if not enabledValue then
		return { mountGui = mountGui }
	end

	local CollectionService = game:GetService("CollectionService")
	local RunService = game:GetService("RunService")

	-- Forward-declared locals (declared fix #1, see module header):
	-- `registerFrame` below wires click handlers that call these before
	-- they're assigned further down. Because they're already `local` here,
	-- the later `name = function(...) end` assignments fill in these same
	-- locals (proper lexical closure) rather than leaking accidental
	-- globals the way v1's un-`local`'d `function name()` declarations did.
	local refreshAllMuteIcons
	local applyGroupVolume
	local nextTrack

	-- Active track lives inside the Music group so it respects its volume.
	local song = Instance.new("Sound")
	song.Name = "ActiveTrack"
	song.SoundGroup = musicGroup :: any
	song.Looped = false
	song.Parent = musicGroup
	trove:Add(song)

	local State = {
		Index = 1,
		Songs = {} :: { Instance },
		-- true while no track is chosen (the playlist was empty at the last
		-- playIndex): the next track that arrives starts the music.
		Idle = true,
		-- bumped by every playIndex: a late Loaded or check of an older track
		-- never restarts the current one.
		Token = 0,
		Muted = (musicGroup :: any).Volume == 0,
		PreMuteVolume = (musicGroup :: any).Volume &gt; 0 and (musicGroup :: any).Volume or 0.5,
	}

	-- ── Frame registry ──────────────────────────────────────────────
	local frames = {} -- { { container = ..., buttons = ... }, ... }
	local wiredFrames = {} -- set of already-wired Frame instances

	local playerGui = game:GetService("Players").LocalPlayer:WaitForChild("PlayerGui")
	local function updateMuteIcon(button)
		local icon = button and button:FindFirstChild("Icon")
		if not icon then return end
		local mutedMark = button:FindFirstChild("MutedMark")
		if button:GetAttribute("PreserveMusicIcon") then
			if mutedMark then mutedMark.Visible = State.Muted end
		else
			icon.Image = State.Muted and ICONS.Muted or ICONS.Playing
		end
	end

	local function registerFrame(frame: Instance)
		if not frame:IsDescendantOf(playerGui) or wiredFrames[frame] then
			return
		end
		local container = frame:FindFirstChild("Container")
		if not container then
			return
		end
		local buttons = container:FindFirstChild("Buttons")
		if not buttons then
			return
		end

		local entry = { container = container, buttons = buttons, scope = trove:Extend() }
		table.insert(frames, entry)
		wiredFrames[frame] = entry

		local muteBtn = buttons:FindFirstChild("Mute")
		if muteBtn then
			entry.scope:Connect((muteBtn :: any).Activated, function()
				if State.Muted then
					State.Muted = false
				else
					State.PreMuteVolume = (musicGroup :: any).Volume
					State.Muted = true
				end
				refreshAllMuteIcons()
				applyGroupVolume()
			end)
		end

		local nextBtn = buttons:FindFirstChild("Next")
		if nextBtn then
			entry.scope:Connect((nextBtn :: any).Activated, function()
				nextTrack(1)
			end)
		end

		-- Sync current state to the new frame immediately.
		local nameLabel = container:FindFirstChild("SongName")
		if nameLabel then
			local e = State.Songs[State.Index]
			;(nameLabel :: any).Text = e and e.Name or "-"
		end

		updateMuteIcon(muteBtn)
	end

	local function unregisterFrame(frame: Instance)
		local entry = wiredFrames[frame]
		if not entry then
			return
		end
		entry.scope:Destroy()
		wiredFrames[frame] = nil
		for i, f in ipairs(frames) do
			if f == entry then
				table.remove(frames, i)
				break
			end
		end
	end

	-- ── Update all frames ───────────────────────────────────────────
	refreshAllMuteIcons = function()
		for _, f in ipairs(frames) do
			updateMuteIcon(f.buttons:FindFirstChild("Mute"))
		end
	end

	local function setAllSongName()
		local e = State.Songs[State.Index]
		local name = (e and e.Name) or "-"
		for _, f in ipairs(frames) do
			local label = f.container:FindFirstChild("SongName")
			if label then
				(label :: any).Text = name
			end
		end
	end

	local function setAllDuration(text: string)
		for _, f in ipairs(frames) do
			local label = f.container:FindFirstChild("Duration")
			if label then
				(label :: any).Text = text
			end
		end
	end

	-- ── Playback ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	applyGroupVolume = function()
		(musicGroup :: any).Volume = State.Muted and 0 or State.PreMuteVolume
	end

	local function rebuildSongList()
		State.Songs = MusicSystem.buildPlaylist(musicGroup, song.Name)
	end

	local function playIndex(index: number)
		local resolved = MusicSystem.nextIndex(index, #State.Songs, 0)
		if resolved == nil then
			State.Idle = true
			setAllSongName()
			setAllDuration("--:-- - --:--")
			song:Stop()
			return
		end
		State.Idle = false
		State.Index = resolved
		local entry = State.Songs[State.Index] :: any
		State.Token += 1
		local token = State.Token
		song.SoundId = MusicSystem.getSoundUri(entry)
		song.TimePosition = 0
		setAllSongName()
		song:Play()
		-- Play on a track that is still loading can be lost: play it again
		-- once it loads, and check once more half a second later.
		local function ensurePlaying()
			if alive and token == State.Token and not State.Idle and not song.IsPlaying then
				song:Play()
			end
		end
		if not song.IsLoaded then
			trove:Add(song.Loaded:Once(ensurePlaying))
		end
		task.delay(0.5, ensurePlaying)
	end

	nextTrack = function(step: number?)
		playIndex(State.Index + (step or 1))
	end

	-- ── Connections ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
	-- Roblox ignores a Play made inside the Sound's own Ended handlers: switch
	-- on the next frame.
	trove:Connect(song.Ended, function()
		task.defer(function()
			if alive then
				nextTrack(1)
			end
		end)
	end)

	-- Sync mute icon when musicGroup.Volume is changed externally (e.g. the
	-- Settings menu via VolumeHandlers).
	-- Any audible level is the one the player's Mute restores: a Settings
	-- volume step while unmuted, then the Settings toggle off, then this Mute
	-- used to bring back the level from before the step.
	trove:Connect((musicGroup :: any):GetPropertyChangedSignal("Volume"), function()
		local isMuted = (musicGroup :: any).Volume == 0
		if not isMuted then
			State.PreMuteVolume = (musicGroup :: any).Volume
		end
		if isMuted == State.Muted then
			return
		end
		State.Muted = isMuted
		refreshAllMuteIcons()
	end)

	-- Throttled time display (~5x/sec).
	local accum = 0
	trove:Connect(RunService.RenderStepped, function(dt: number)
		accum += dt
		if accum &lt; 0.2 then
			return
		end
		accum = 0
		local pos, len = song.TimePosition, song.TimeLength
		if len ~= math.huge and len &gt; 0 then
			setAllDuration(string.format("%s - %s", formatMMSS(pos), formatMMSS(len)))
		else
			setAllDuration(string.format("%s - --:--", formatMMSS(pos)))
		end
	end)

	-- Keep the song list in sync with the Music group.
	trove:Connect(musicGroup.ChildAdded, function(child: Instance)
		task.wait()
		if
			child:IsA("Sound")
			and child.Name ~= song.Name
			and MusicSystem.getSoundUri(child) ~= ""
		then
			table.insert(State.Songs, child)
			-- Tracks that replicate after the client started: the first one in
			-- starts the music, on a random track of those already here (a
			-- batch arrives together, so one frame later it is all here).
			if State.Idle then
				State.Index = math.random(1, #State.Songs)
				playIndex(State.Index)
			end
		end
	end)
	trove:Connect(musicGroup.ChildRemoved, function(child: Instance)
		if child:IsA("Sound") then
			for i, s in ipairs(State.Songs) do
				if s.Name == child.Name then
					table.remove(State.Songs, i)
					break
				end
			end
		end
	end)

	-- Only live PlayerGui views share playback. Stored templates must stay
	-- pristine; reparented/late UI is discovered through DescendantAdded too.
	trove:Connect(playerGui.DescendantAdded, function(frame)
		if frame:HasTag("MusicPlayerFrame") then registerFrame(frame) end
	end)
	trove:Connect(playerGui.DescendantRemoving, unregisterFrame)
	for _, frame in ipairs(CollectionService:GetTagged("MusicPlayerFrame")) do
		registerFrame(frame)
	end
	trove:Connect(CollectionService:GetInstanceAddedSignal("MusicPlayerFrame"), registerFrame)
	trove:Connect(CollectionService:GetInstanceRemovedSignal("MusicPlayerFrame"), unregisterFrame)

	-- ── Init ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	rebuildSongList()
	if #State.Songs &gt; 0 then
		State.Index = math.random(1, #State.Songs)
	end
	refreshAllMuteIcons()
	applyGroupVolume()
	playIndex(State.Index)
	return { mountGui = mountGui }
end

return MusicSystem
</string>
                <int64 name="SourceAssetId">-1</int64>
                <BinaryString name="Tags" />
              </Properties>
            </Item>
            <Item class="ModuleScript" referent="30">
              <Properties>
                <string name="Name">OneShots</string>
                <BinaryString name="AttributesSerialize" />
                <SecurityCapabilities name="Capabilities">0</SecurityCapabilities>
                <ContentId name="LinkedSource">
                  <null>
                  </null>
                </ContentId>
                <bool name="DefinesCapabilities">false</bool>
                <BinaryString name="SerializedOverrides">
                </BinaryString>
                <string name="Source">-- Optional per-play mix controls, without mutating authored templates or Kernel.
local Debris=game:GetService('Debris')
local OneShots={};OneShots.__index=OneShots
function OneShots.new(group,enabled)
 local self=setmetatable({group=group,enabled=enabled,voices={},count=0,dead=false},OneShots)
 self.connection=enabled.Changed:Connect(function()if not enabled.Value then self:stopAll()end end)
 return self
end
function OneShots:stopAll()
 local copy={};for sound in self.voices do table.insert(copy,sound)end
 for _,sound in copy do sound:Destroy()end
end
function OneShots:play(name,options)
 if self.dead or not self.enabled.Value or self.group.Volume&lt;=0 or self.count&gt;=8 then return nil end
 local source=self.group:FindFirstChild(name)
 if not source or not source:IsA('Sound') or not source.IsLoaded then return nil end
 options=options or {}
 local sound=source:Clone();sound.Name='OneShot_'..name;sound.Looped=false
 sound.Volume=source.Volume*math.clamp(tonumber(options.volume) or 1,0,2)
 sound.PlaybackSpeed=math.clamp(tonumber(options.pitch) or source.PlaybackSpeed,.5,2)
 sound.SoundGroup=self.group;sound.TimePosition=0
 self.voices[sound]=true;self.count+=1
 sound.Destroying:Once(function()if self.voices[sound]then self.voices[sound]=nil;self.count-=1 end end)
 sound.Ended:Once(function()sound:Destroy()end)
 sound.Parent=self.group
 -- Failed playback/moderation must not leak a clone waiting forever for Ended.
 Debris:AddItem(sound,8);sound:Play()
 return sound
end
function OneShots:destroy()
 if self.dead then return end
 self.dead=true;self.connection:Disconnect();self:stopAll()
end
return OneShots
</string>
